From: Ferestre contributors
Subject: Keep token requests off title queues, answer roles freshly after a quiet spell, and verify server certificates

A token request's DoWork no longer makes the service round trip itself. In
strict manual mode (XGR_STRICT_MANUAL_QUEUES, Halo Infinite) DoWork runs on
the title's own Work port, so with the service stalled the title's dispatch
thread was held for the whole exchange, up to 65 s, and its own work queued
behind it. DoWork now hands the exchange to the pool, or a thread of its own,
through xgr_async_run_detached, which keeps it counted as the queue's work:
terminating the queue still waits for it, and the terminated callback cannot
overtake its completion.

The role poller stops 30 s after the last question, so the first
XUserGetState, XUserIsStoreUser or XStoreIsLicenseValid after a quiet spell
used to answer from the end of the last window: a licence still valid after a
Store sign-out, a player still signed in after signing out. Such a question
now wakes the poller and waits for its answer, for at most 1 s; a service that
does not answer in time gets the last state, as during any outage.
Registration and XUserAddAsync are unchanged.

XNetworkingVerifyServerCertificate accepted every certificate. It now takes
the server certificate from the WinHTTP request handle and verifies its chain
against the request's host under the SSL policy, as WinHTTP does while
connecting, honouring the SECURITY_FLAG_IGNORE_* exceptions the request
carries. A handle that is not a WinHTTP request, or a request with no server
certificate, keeps the old answer and is logged once. winhttp and crypt32 are
looked up at run time, not imported.

